# gitleaks fingerprint allowlist (E5-T3).
#
# Each entry is <commit-sha>:<path>:<rule-id>:<line> and suppresses exactly one
# finding in one historical commit. Nothing else is suppressed: a new secret, the
# same shape on another line, or the same line reintroduced by another commit
# produces a different fingerprint and still fails the `secret-scan` job.
#
# All three entries are synthetic, non-functional literals committed as unit-test
# inputs for the redaction helper (`redact`), which exists to keep such shapes out
# of logs. They are not credentials, reference no real tenant/account/endpoint, and
# grant no access. They live in already-published history and therefore cannot be
# removed by editing the working tree; suppressing them by fingerprint is the only
# way to keep history scanning enabled rather than disabling the scan.
